development for Apple

00:01:12.058 --> 00:01:16.171
and comes at a reported
cost of over $200 million.

00:01:16.171 --> 00:01:17.383
- Yeah, I think $200 million 

00:01:17.383 --> 00:01:20.133
is more than World War
Two cost back in the day.

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:21.583
So we're recreating it.

00:01:21.583 --> 00:01:23.361
But you're right, you had me at air force.

00:01:23.361 --> 00:01:25.831
Because 'Band of Brothers'
we got to see the army,

00:01:25.831 --> 00:01:27.503
then with 'The Pacific' we got the marines.

00:01:27.503 --> 00:01:31.506
And to see an air force
unit go against Germany.

00:01:31.506 --> 00:01:33.717
To see the dog fighting up close

00:01:33.717 --> 00:01:35.902
is what I'm hoping we're gonna
get to see. So this is like,

00:01:35.902 --> 00:01:38.470
between 'Top Gun Maverick'
next year and this,

00:01:38.470 --> 00:01:40.451
there's gonna be a lot of
great aerial combat to watch.

00:01:40.451 --> 00:01:41.451
- That's true!
- I know!

00:01:41.451 --> 00:01:43.078
Who doesn't like aerial combat?

00:01:43.078 --> 00:01:43.911
- It's the best.

00:01:43.911 --> 00:01:46.693
- All right guys, second story
is Jennifer Aniston is putting

00:01:46.693 --> 00:01:49.172
those 'Friends' reboot rumors to rest.

00:01:49.172 --> 00:01:50.784
Erin, contain yourself.

00:01:50.784 --> 00:01:52.353
In a new interview with Variety,

00:01:52.353 --> 00:01:53.702
Aniston revealed that even though

00:01:53.702 --> 00:01:55.452
there's been an interest
in a 'Friends' movie,

00:01:55.452 --> 00:01:58.422
the producers of the sitcom won't allow it

00:01:58.422 --> 00:01:59.972
- I get it. Kind of.

00:01:59.972 --> 00:02:02.521
I mean, I love Friends so much,

00:02:02.521 --> 00:02:05.460
I will watch anything that
they produce of 'Friends.'

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:06.764
But also at the same time as a fan,

00:02:06.764 --> 00:02:09.162
I'm not clambering for that.

00:02:09.162 --> 00:02:10.852
I don't need a movie of it.

00:02:10.852 --> 00:02:12.322
I feel like the 10 seasons,

00:02:12.322 --> 00:02:13.503
10 seasons,

00:02:13.503 --> 00:02:14.424
are enough.

00:02:14.424 --> 00:02:15.257
- [Naz] Yeah.

00:02:15.257 --> 00:02:17.632
- Don't we remember in like 2002, and 2003

00:02:17.632 --> 00:02:18.465
when we were like,

00:02:18.465 --> 00:02:20.065
"Can we just get to the finish line?

00:02:20.065 --> 00:02:21.443
(Naz laughs)

00:02:21.443 --> 00:02:22.747
"Can Ross and Rachel get together

00:02:22.747 --> 00:02:24.438
"and have the monkey walk down the aisle

00:02:24.438 --> 00:02:25.773
"and give them the ring?

00:02:25.773 --> 00:02:26.795
"Let's get this thing done."

00:02:26.795 --> 00:02:29.262
And now I'm at my favorite
coffee shop two weeks ago

00:02:29.262 --> 00:02:31.844
and they're celebrating
'Friends'' 25th anniversary.

00:02:31.844 --> 00:02:33.102
- I know.
- It's everywhere.

00:02:33.102 --> 00:02:33.993
- It's all over the,

00:02:33.993 --> 00:02:37.019
when did we start celebrating the 25th?

00:02:37.019 --> 00:02:38.554
Did we have a 25th anniversary for 'Cheers'?

00:02:38.554 --> 00:02:39.387
- No!

00:02:39.387 --> 00:02:41.194
We're very nostalgic now.

00:02:41.194 --> 00:02:43.464
We just live in a very nostalgic timeline.

00:02:43.464 --> 00:02:45.130
- It's so true, it's like
we're living in the past.

00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:46.627
We're not even living
in the present anymore.

00:02:46.627 --> 00:02:48.997
It is a shame, I did want
a remix to Smelly Cat,

00:02:48.997 --> 00:02:50.314
but there we are guys.

00:02:50.314 --> 00:02:51.496
Alright, and finally,

00:02:51.496 --> 00:02:54.402
Amazon has released a trailer
for its hit Emmy winner

00:02:54.402 --> 00:02:55.534
'The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel'

00:02:55.534 --> 00:02:57.855
which returns in December
for season three.

00:02:57.855 --> 00:02:59.960
Erin, we have talked about this so much.

00:02:59.960 --> 00:03:02.106
This show just wants
to be a musical so bad.

00:03:02.106 --> 00:03:04.351
- It's screaming to be a musical.

00:03:04.351 --> 00:03:06.492
And in the trailer we have Sutton Foster

00:03:06.492 --> 00:03:07.983
singing over everything.

00:03:07.983 --> 00:03:08.816
And I'm like,

00:03:08.816 --> 00:03:10.182
"Just be a musical, it's OK buddy.

00:03:10.182 --> 00:03:11.120
"I love you!

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:14.292
"Like, I'll love you as a
musical too, it's fine."

00:03:14.292 --> 00:03:16.372
- I was so stoked to see
Sterling K. Brown in it.

00:03:16.372 --> 00:03:17.223
- Me too.
- But sad

00:03:17.223 --> 00:03:19.412
that I did not see Zachary
Levi in the trailer.

00:03:19.412 --> 00:03:21.736
- I am just excited to watch this show

00:03:21.736 --> 00:03:22.826
because what I love about it

00:03:22.826 --> 00:03:25.516
is that it really feels
like it gets the time right,

00:03:25.516 --> 00:03:27.364
as far as what standup comics were doing,

00:03:27.364 --> 00:03:28.457
what kind of shows they were doing.

00:03:28.457 --> 00:03:29.686
And so now she's going on the road,

00:03:29.686 --> 00:03:31.542
she's opening for a huge music act.

00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:34.712
And this is what it's like to
be a comic that is featuring.

00:03:34.712 --> 00:03:36.222
Where you want to get to the next level.

00:03:36.222 --> 00:03:37.360
You know you're not the headliner,

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:39.382
so you're trying to get as much knowledge,

00:03:39.382 --> 00:03:41.400
you're trying to learn
all the different cities,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:42.233
all the different routes,

00:03:42.233 --> 00:03:43.600
who can be booking you in the future.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:45.072
That's what I love about 'Mrs. Maisel'

00:03:45.072 --> 00:03:48.039
is the authenticity to
standup back in the day.

00:03:48.039 --> 00:03:48.872
- All right friends,

00:03:48.872 --> 00:03:50.661
those are our week's top stories.

00:03:50.661 --> 00:03:52.501
But before we dive deep into 'El Camino,'

00:03:52.501 --> 00:03:54.149
Erin and I actually took a quick moment

00:03:54.149 --> 00:03:56.120
to discuss how we choose
what to watch and why

00:03:56.120 --> 00:03:58.101
because there are so many
good options out there.

00:03:58.101 --> 00:03:59.678
Check it out.

00:03:59.678 --> 00:04:02.112
Erin, is is hard for you to
pick a show to watch these days?

00:04:02.112 --> 00:04:03.973
I feel like every day
there's a new show coming out

00:04:03.973 --> 00:04:06.084
and I'm constantly adding
shows to my watch list.

00:04:06.084 --> 00:04:07.093
- Oh, totally.

00:04:07.093 --> 00:04:10.113
I'm always scrolling social
media and it's between

00:04:10.113 --> 00:04:12.175
what the critics want
you to watch and fans.

00:04:12.175 --> 00:04:13.238
It's so much stuff.

00:04:13.238 --> 00:04:14.071
- It's overwhelming.

00:04:14.071 --> 00:04:15.955
Everyday I'm like, what
are my coworkers watching?

00:04:15.955 --> 00:04:16.857
What are my friends watching?

00:04:16.857 --> 00:04:18.736
Sometimes I think, what's
my future husband watching?

00:04:18.736 --> 00:04:21.868
I just wish it came easier
to me, like eating does.

00:04:21.868 --> 00:04:22.951
'Cause when it comes to eating,

00:04:22.951 --> 00:04:24.282
my go-to is Freschetta.

00:04:24.282 --> 00:04:25.291
'Cause when I'm eating Freschetta,

00:04:25.291 --> 00:04:28.231
I never have to limit myself
to one kind or one slice.

00:04:28.231 --> 00:04:30.362
- Also, I really like to
pair my pizza toppings

00:04:30.362 --> 00:04:31.303
with what I'm watching.

00:04:31.303 --> 00:04:32.969
- Ooh.
- So it's a Hawaiian pizza

00:04:32.969 --> 00:04:34.955
if I'm in the mood for an action comedy.

00:04:34.955 --> 00:04:35.812
- That's fun.
- And then it's

00:04:35.812 --> 00:04:36.750
the brick oven five cheese

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:38.510
if I want something a little more serious.

00:04:38.510 --> 00:04:39.490
- Oh my God, full disclosure,

00:04:39.490 --> 00:04:41.392
I eat the brick oven five
cheese when I'm home alone

00:04:41.392 --> 00:04:42.983
so I can eat the entire thing by myself.

00:04:42.983 --> 00:04:44.649
That's how delicious it is.

00:04:44.649 --> 00:04:46.315
- Totally. I'm here for that as well.

00:04:46.315 --> 00:04:47.148
Can we eat?

00:04:47.148 --> 00:04:48.545
- Oh my God, I was hoping you'd ask!

00:04:48.545 --> 00:04:49.545
Let's do it.

00:04:50.496 --> 00:04:53.177
It is time to talk 'El
Camino: A Breaking Bad Movie.'

00:04:53.177 --> 00:04:55.239
Written and directed by Vince Gilligan.

00:04:55.239 --> 00:04:58.088
It picks up right where the
series ended six years ago.

00:04:58.088 --> 00:04:58.921
Crazy.

00:04:58.921 --> 00:05:00.497
And the film is currently Certified Fresh

00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:02.677
with a a 94% on the tomato meter

00:05:02.677 --> 00:05:04.026
at the time of this recording.

00:05:04.026 --> 00:05:05.046
And it's really just what

00:05:05.046 --> 00:05:06.774
everyone has been talking
about over the weekend.

00:05:06.774 --> 00:05:08.284
Also, just know

00:05:08.284 --> 00:05:09.716
this is your official spoiler alert.

00:05:09.716 --> 00:05:12.094
So if you haven't seen
'Breaking Bad' or 'El Camino,'

00:05:12.094 --> 00:05:13.812
do not @ us.

00:05:13.812 --> 00:05:15.132
But I am so excited to talk about

00:05:15.132 --> 00:05:16.854
'El Camino: A Breaking
Bad Movie' with you guys.

00:05:16.854 --> 00:05:20.276
Do you think that fans got
the closure they deserved

00:05:20.276 --> 00:05:23.247
and do you think Jesse got
the ending that he needed?

00:05:23.247 --> 00:05:26.033
- I think Jesse got the
ending that he needed

00:05:26.033 --> 00:05:28.024
but I also think that he got an ending

00:05:28.024 --> 00:05:31.275
that I had already filled in for him.

00:05:31.275 --> 00:05:33.613
At the end of 'Breaking Bad'
he has this great moment

00:05:33.613 --> 00:05:35.765
and he's driving away in
the car, in the El Camino,

00:05:35.765 --> 00:05:37.542
and he's so,

00:05:37.542 --> 00:05:39.151
you realize that he is free.

00:05:39.151 --> 00:05:40.101
He's escaped.

00:05:40.101 --> 00:05:40.934
- Right.

00:05:40.934 --> 00:05:43.256
- So I sort of filled in
as a viewer, as a fan,

00:05:43.256 --> 00:05:45.376
what the movie told us happened.

00:05:45.376 --> 00:05:47.627
So it's fun to go and watch him

00:05:47.627 --> 00:05:49.037
and watch him go through all that

00:05:49.037 --> 00:05:51.235
and watch him get to Alaska,

00:05:51.235 --> 00:05:53.117
but I also sort of,

00:05:53.117 --> 00:05:56.237
I sort of wanted the movie to
be two years in the future,

00:05:56.237 --> 00:05:58.973
he's just set up in Alaska living there,

00:05:58.973 --> 00:06:00.605
to see what his new life is.

00:06:00.605 --> 00:06:01.870
Because for me,

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:05.019
I got closure from Jesse's
character from the series.

00:06:05.019 --> 00:06:06.059
- Really?
- I think that's

00:06:06.059 --> 00:06:07.366
the trick with this movie is that because,

00:06:07.366 --> 00:06:09.978
we are such big 'Breaking Bad' fans

00:06:09.978 --> 00:06:12.766
that you're going to create
these narratives in your head.

00:06:12.766 --> 00:06:14.268
You're gonna write your own fan fiction

00:06:14.268 --> 00:06:15.618
before this movie comes out.

00:06:15.618 --> 00:06:18.819
So I do think it was important
to actually watch Jesse

00:06:18.819 --> 00:06:21.449
matriculate from his
situation in Albuquerque

00:06:21.449 --> 00:06:22.870
to get to Alaska.

00:06:22.870 --> 00:06:25.521
And there is some nice
surprises within that narrative,

00:06:25.521 --> 00:06:26.659
but overall, yeah.

00:06:26.659 --> 00:06:28.505
I think we assumed he
was gonna get somewhere,

00:06:28.505 --> 00:06:30.475
and so what this movie did is,

00:06:30.475 --> 00:06:33.365
yes it satisfied my
appetite for 'Breaking Bad'

00:06:33.365 --> 00:06:34.456
but now I want more.

00:06:34.456 --> 00:06:36.755
And I wanna see that
movie two years from now,

00:06:36.755 --> 00:06:38.503
what is Jesse up to in Alaska?

00:06:38.503 --> 00:06:40.105
Is he also working in a Cinnabon?

00:06:40.105 --> 00:06:41.746
(Naz laughs)
- Exactly.

00:06:41.746 --> 00:06:42.579
- Like Saul.
- Exactly.

00:06:42.579 --> 00:06:43.412
- We always want more.

00:06:43.412 --> 00:06:44.245
We are never satisfied.

00:06:44.245 --> 00:06:46.491
I've gotta say, I actually
was totally satisfied,

00:06:46.491 --> 00:06:47.385
and I'm with you, Mark.

00:06:47.385 --> 00:06:49.594
I just think Jesse had
been through so much.

00:06:49.594 --> 00:06:52.006
He was the character that
I related to the most.

00:06:52.006 --> 00:06:53.226
He's the one that had a soul.

00:06:53.226 --> 00:06:55.177
When he was throwing the
blood money out the window

00:06:55.177 --> 00:06:56.206
in the final season.

00:06:56.206 --> 00:06:57.775
Giving it to random people.

00:06:57.775 --> 00:07:00.108
I feel like he never got
his redemption story,

00:07:00.108 --> 00:07:01.838
unlike the other main characters,

00:07:01.838 --> 00:07:03.326
who either all died,

00:07:03.326 --> 00:07:05.358
or we kinda knew what
was gonna happen to them

00:07:05.358 --> 00:07:06.297
months later, you know?

00:07:06.297 --> 00:07:07.766
Like, all right, this kid's gonna be fine.

00:07:07.766 --> 00:07:08.846
He's gonna have a lot of money.

00:07:08.846 --> 00:07:10.308
That never happened for Jesse.

00:07:10.308 --> 00:07:13.564
And for Vince to just write
this for us so beautifully

00:07:13.564 --> 00:07:15.108
and call it 'El Camino,'

00:07:15.108 --> 00:07:17.067
not just after the car,

00:07:17.067 --> 00:07:19.348
but "El Camino" actually
means journey and path.

00:07:19.348 --> 00:07:21.628
So I actually really liked
seeing his path to Alaska.

00:07:21.628 --> 00:07:25.046
And just how he got a chance
to properly say goodbye

00:07:25.046 --> 00:07:27.066
to all these people that he
never really got a chance

00:07:27.066 --> 00:07:28.726
to properly say goodbye to.

00:07:28.726 --> 00:07:31.057
You know, like Mike and Walt.

00:07:31.057 --> 00:07:33.087
Just hearing Walt tell him

00:07:33.087 --> 00:07:35.227
you would have amounted to something,

00:07:35.227 --> 00:07:38.578
I was so happy to see all
these characters come back.

00:07:38.578 --> 00:07:40.047
Who were you guys happy to see come back?

00:07:40.047 --> 00:07:42.632
- I was happy to see
everyone, pretty much.

00:07:42.632 --> 00:07:43.613
I was just like,

00:07:43.613 --> 00:07:44.592
oh this is nice.

00:07:44.592 --> 00:07:46.261
I mean, it's nice, it's fun.

00:07:46.261 --> 00:07:47.199
It's what Mark was saying,

00:07:47.199 --> 00:07:49.900
it's fun as a fan to see
more stuff in the world.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:52.060
You're just like, oh great
I love these characters.

00:07:52.060 --> 00:07:53.319
What are you guys doing?

00:07:53.319 --> 00:07:54.260
This is wonderful.

00:07:54.260 --> 00:07:56.949
Even the flashbacks that are terrible,

00:07:56.949 --> 00:07:58.943
Jesse's going through
such horrible things.

00:07:58.943 --> 00:08:01.032
It's just like, oh, this is so fun.

00:08:01.032 --> 00:08:04.475
These are my friends
and I like seeing them.

00:08:04.475 --> 00:08:05.799
It's fun to see all those people.

00:08:05.799 --> 00:08:06.660
I think it's,

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:07.729
it was weird to see Walter

00:08:07.729 --> 00:08:09.510
because at the end of the series you end

00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:11.260
and he is a super villain.

00:08:11.260 --> 00:08:13.369
He has just become like,

00:08:13.369 --> 00:08:15.260
this crazy super villain.

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.609
And it's like, we don't have that aspect

00:08:17.609 --> 00:08:19.559
in this movie at all, obviously.

00:08:19.559 --> 00:08:20.649
Because he's dead.

00:08:20.649 --> 00:08:23.778
And then to go back and
see this very tender moment

00:08:23.778 --> 00:08:25.901
was sort of like, oh yeah.

00:08:25.901 --> 00:08:27.061
They were buddies.

00:08:27.061 --> 00:08:28.119
- They do love each other.
- Yeah!

00:08:28.119 --> 00:08:29.648
They were friends at one point!

00:08:29.648 --> 00:08:30.719
- And he's bald again!

00:08:30.719 --> 00:08:32.471
Good job Bryan Cranston.

00:08:32.471 --> 00:08:34.398
- His head's a little misshapen in this

00:08:34.398 --> 00:08:35.901
but hey, we'll forgive that.

00:08:35.901 --> 00:08:37.399
- Just a little!
- What was really nice for me

00:08:37.399 --> 00:08:39.911
was we got to see one last great

00:08:39.911 --> 00:08:42.382
Robert Forster performance.
- [Naz And Erin] Yes.

00:08:42.382 --> 00:08:43.719
- [Mark] Because seeing him it's like,

00:08:43.719 --> 00:08:46.527
and he literally passed away
the day the movie came out.

00:08:46.527 --> 00:08:47.510
- Which is crazy.

00:08:47.510 --> 00:08:48.927
From brain cancer, so sad.

00:08:48.927 --> 00:08:49.779
- So wild.

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:50.612
- But he was great.

00:08:50.612 --> 00:08:52.801
And really the star of this movie to me,

00:08:52.801 --> 00:08:54.779
as great as Aaron Paul
was, was Vince Gilligan.

00:08:54.779 --> 00:08:55.612
- Oh, okay.

00:08:55.612 --> 00:08:56.445
- It was Vince Gilligan

00:08:56.445 --> 00:08:57.310
because the filmmaking style,

00:08:57.310 --> 00:08:58.350
that's what I miss.

00:08:58.350 --> 00:08:59.961
You don't see that in television,

00:08:59.961 --> 00:09:01.030
or film for that matter,

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:03.343
so there's just certain
shots that you know is just

00:09:03.343 --> 00:09:05.173
Vince Gilligan has had this shot forever.

00:09:05.173 --> 00:09:07.133
The shot of Jesse trying to find the money

00:09:07.133 --> 00:09:08.963
in every room in the house.

00:09:08.963 --> 00:09:10.236
That was so cool to me.

00:09:10.236 --> 00:09:11.975
That's what I miss from Breaking Bad.

00:09:11.975 --> 00:09:12.808
- I know.

00:09:12.808 --> 00:09:14.346
And it reminds us why
we love 'Breaking Bad,'

00:09:14.346 --> 00:09:15.656
because of the way it looked,

00:09:15.656 --> 00:09:16.857
and how there was nothing else

00:09:16.857 --> 00:09:18.327
on television like it at the time.

00:09:18.327 --> 00:09:19.160
I agree with you.

00:09:19.160 --> 00:09:20.428
But when you said star of the film,

00:09:20.428 --> 00:09:22.198
I thought you were
gonna say Jesse Plemons,

00:09:22.198 --> 00:09:24.257
because Todd, guys!

00:09:24.257 --> 00:09:28.040
Todd singing Dr Hook,
"Sharing the Night Together,"

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:28.873
to me,

00:09:28.873 --> 00:09:30.373
I watched it like three times.

00:09:30.373 --> 00:09:33.505
He is so good at just playing
such a good sociopath.

00:09:33.505 --> 00:09:35.785
I think it's because his
eyes are all black and beady,

00:09:35.785 --> 00:09:37.343
he looks like a shark almost.

00:09:37.343 --> 00:09:39.508
- And he had this little
moment of humanity,

00:09:39.508 --> 00:09:41.038
which I didn't know Todd had in him.

00:09:41.038 --> 00:09:41.871
- Right.

00:09:41.871 --> 00:09:42.704
- Humanity? Which moment?

00:09:42.704 --> 00:09:43.537
- When Jesse,

00:09:43.537 --> 00:09:44.370
he's being tortured,

00:09:44.370 --> 00:09:47.588
and because Jesse had given
the gun back to him earlier,

00:09:47.588 --> 00:09:48.689
he's just thinking,

00:09:48.689 --> 00:09:50.036
"Man, I feel bad for this kid.

00:09:50.036 --> 00:09:51.089
"Can we cut this?"

00:09:51.089 --> 00:09:52.929
It's like, wow even Todd is a person!

00:09:52.929 --> 00:09:55.728
- I saw him do that in
the final season two,

00:09:55.728 --> 00:09:57.388
when he brought him Ben &amp; Jerry's.

00:09:57.388 --> 00:09:58.788
When he brought him ice cream.
- Little bit, yeah.

00:09:58.788 --> 00:09:59.621
- So you would always see that.

00:09:59.621 --> 00:10:01.101
But yeah, how he would
talk about the housekeeper

00:10:01.101 --> 00:10:02.363
was just so creepy.

00:10:02.363 --> 00:10:03.196
And then he's like,

00:10:03.196 --> 00:10:05.422
"Oh no, excellent house keeper."

00:10:05.422 --> 00:10:06.682
It was just so funny.

00:10:06.682 --> 00:10:09.597
I want more Dr Hook, definitely.

00:10:09.597 --> 00:10:10.430
More Dr Hook.

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:11.979
But that's also what Vince is so good at,

00:10:11.979 --> 00:10:14.450
is putting the best music and that stuff.

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:15.567
- [Erin] Yeah, that's true.

00:10:15.567 --> 00:10:17.160
- But I want to ask you guys,

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:18.934
on other TV series,

00:10:18.934 --> 00:10:20.717
like 'Sopranos' and 'Game of Thrones,'

00:10:20.717 --> 00:10:23.747
the final season of 'Breaking
Bad' is critically acclaimed.

00:10:23.747 --> 00:10:26.976
In fact, 'Breaking Bad' seasons
two, three, four, and five

00:10:26.976 --> 00:10:29.168
all 100% on the Tomatometer.

00:10:29.168 --> 00:10:31.696
So one would argue that
it was definitely a risk

00:10:31.696 --> 00:10:34.917
to go back to this
story and this plot line

00:10:34.917 --> 00:10:37.237
and just touch something
that's so perfect.

00:10:37.237 --> 00:10:39.823
So, do you guys think this
movie needed to be made?

00:10:39.823 --> 00:10:42.943
Or should Jesse's fate
kinda been left open ended?

00:10:42.943 --> 00:10:45.368
- I don't think it needed to be made.

00:10:45.368 --> 00:10:47.244
But I'm not mad that it was made.

00:10:47.244 --> 00:10:48.646
Like (Erin laughs)

00:10:48.646 --> 00:10:51.020
you know, I enjoy watching it.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:52.544
I enjoy that thing,

00:10:52.544 --> 00:10:53.461
that world.

00:10:54.677 --> 00:10:57.065
The finale of 'Breaking Bad' is perfect.

00:10:57.065 --> 00:10:57.898
- Unreal.

00:10:57.898 --> 00:10:59.756
- Like, it's just perfect.

00:10:59.756 --> 00:11:00.906
And it's that moment,

00:11:00.906 --> 00:11:02.666
it's that moment of Jesse driving away.

00:11:02.666 --> 00:11:05.807
I felt that was perfect closure for him.

00:11:05.807 --> 00:11:08.556
You feel that this is what's
gonna happen with him.

00:11:08.556 --> 00:11:10.887
You feel his emotion.

00:11:10.887 --> 00:11:12.997
I didn't think it was
necessary to make this movie

00:11:12.997 --> 00:11:14.743
but I'm not mad about it.

00:11:14.743 --> 00:11:16.114
- Yeah, this wasn't a need.

00:11:16.114 --> 00:11:17.496
This wasn't water for me,

00:11:17.496 --> 00:11:19.312
but it was a really nice soda.

00:11:19.312 --> 00:11:20.925
Like, I don't,

00:11:20.925 --> 00:11:22.305
I don't need this to survive.
- nice cold soda.

00:11:22.305 --> 00:11:24.033
- But oh God, is it refreshing.

00:11:24.033 --> 00:11:25.444
And I think the reason why we got this

00:11:25.444 --> 00:11:26.774
and why it was less of a risk

00:11:26.774 --> 00:11:28.688
was that we have 'Better Call Saul' too,

00:11:28.688 --> 00:11:30.409
so we'd been back to this world before,

00:11:30.409 --> 00:11:31.691
and the fans have proven

00:11:31.691 --> 00:11:33.714
that we're gonna accept
different characters

00:11:33.714 --> 00:11:36.611
that maybe have ancillary
ties to 'Breaking Bad.'

00:11:36.611 --> 00:11:38.452
We have one character that's a gateway

00:11:38.452 --> 00:11:39.628
into a different story.

00:11:39.628 --> 00:11:41.342
And so that's why I don't
think that this is gonna be

00:11:41.342 --> 00:11:42.800
the last 'Breaking Bad' movie.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:44.922
I love that we call it 'El
Camino: A Breaking Bad Movie.'

00:11:44.922 --> 00:11:47.252
We could have tons of 'Breaking Bad' movies,

00:11:47.252 --> 00:11:48.942
and I think the fans would be up for it.

00:11:48.942 --> 00:11:51.400
- Yeah, well let the rumors fly!

00:11:51.400 --> 00:11:53.406
Yeah, I'm really happy
that this movie got made

00:11:53.406 --> 00:11:54.747
for all the reasons I said before.

00:11:54.747 --> 00:11:57.576
I really wanted to see
Jesse's story kind of finish.

00:11:57.576 --> 00:11:58.917
But I didn't feel like the movie

00:11:58.917 --> 00:12:00.904
needed to be two hours long.

00:12:00.904 --> 00:12:02.634
My dad called me and I was telling him

00:12:02.634 --> 00:12:03.746
we were doing the show today

00:12:03.746 --> 00:12:05.364
and he's like, "Well,
what's the movie about?

00:12:05.364 --> 00:12:06.197
"What happens?"

00:12:06.197 --> 00:12:09.418
And I was like, "Not much
happens in the movie!

00:12:09.418 --> 00:12:10.796
"But it's beautiful!"

00:12:10.796 --> 00:12:13.028
And I love that Vince kinda
went back to New Mexico

00:12:13.028 --> 00:12:15.422
and we got to see all that scenery again.

00:12:15.422 --> 00:12:17.891
And in fact we actually
sat down with Aaron Paul

00:12:17.891 --> 00:12:19.862
and talked to him about what it was like

00:12:19.862 --> 00:12:20.920
shooting back in New Mexico

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:22.382
and keeping this movie a secret.

00:12:22.382 --> 00:12:23.870
It's great, check it out.

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.308
- To be honest, when we were going

00:12:25.308 --> 00:12:27.959
to set from base camp to set,

00:12:27.959 --> 00:12:31.410
they did have us wear
these big sort of like

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:33.810
Star Wars kind of cloaks

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:34.893
just covering

00:12:36.116 --> 00:12:37.087
our entire bodies

00:12:37.087 --> 00:12:38.837
which felt hilarious.

00:12:40.005 --> 00:12:41.874
But, on the day to day,

00:12:41.874 --> 00:12:43.373
on the weekends,

00:12:43.373 --> 00:12:44.363
I just kind of went out

00:12:44.363 --> 00:12:46.504
and people would come up to me

00:12:46.504 --> 00:12:49.205
and ask for photos or
autographs or whatever

00:12:49.205 --> 00:12:50.504
and ask why I was there

00:12:50.504 --> 00:12:52.443
and I would just lie to them.

00:12:52.443 --> 00:12:53.276
And it was fine,

00:12:53.276 --> 00:12:55.318
no one really second-guessed it.

00:12:55.318 --> 00:12:56.534
- Immediately went on eBay

00:12:56.534 --> 00:12:58.612
and tried to find a
Star Wars looking cloak

00:12:58.612 --> 00:12:59.761
worn by Aaron Paul

00:12:59.761 --> 00:13:01.651
but I found nothing guys.

00:13:01.651 --> 00:13:02.548
Also, who's in New Mexico

00:13:02.548 --> 00:13:04.041
and doesn't think that Aaron Paul is doing

00:13:04.041 --> 00:13:05.220
a 'Breaking Bad' thing?

00:13:05.220 --> 00:13:06.511
- I assume people must have been like,

00:13:06.511 --> 00:13:08.071
"Er, you're lying buddy."

00:13:08.071 --> 00:13:11.380
They must have thought
something was going on.

00:13:11.380 --> 00:13:13.664
- There's just gonna be one
Albuquerque clothing store

00:13:13.664 --> 00:13:14.674
that sells cloaks

00:13:14.674 --> 00:13:17.173
and their business is booming.

00:13:17.173 --> 00:13:19.386
The kids are really
getting back into cloaks,

00:13:19.386 --> 00:13:20.905
it's gonna be a good summer.

00:13:20.905 --> 00:13:22.335
- The Albuquerque cloak factory.

00:13:22.335 --> 00:13:23.168
What you guys got?

00:13:23.168 --> 00:13:26.084
We got cloaks and we got
hazmat suits and that is,

00:13:26.084 --> 00:13:26.917
and vacuums,

00:13:26.917 --> 00:13:28.604
and that is all we have.

00:13:28.604 --> 00:13:31.147
No, but I am so happy
that they made this film.

00:13:31.147 --> 00:13:33.837
And Netflix dropped this
trailer out of nowhere, guys.

00:13:33.837 --> 00:13:34.747
Which is kinda insane.

00:13:34.747 --> 00:13:36.066
None of us were expecting it.

00:13:36.066 --> 00:13:37.847
But 'Breaking Bad' obviously
isn't the only show

00:13:37.847 --> 00:13:38.963
to now get its own film.

00:13:38.963 --> 00:13:39.943
We've seen this recently.

00:13:39.943 --> 00:13:41.924
'Deadwood,' 'Downtown Abbey.'

00:13:41.924 --> 00:13:44.044
So I'm curious to know from you guys

00:13:44.044 --> 00:13:45.844
whether any other longstanding series

00:13:45.844 --> 00:13:47.412
that you guys would love to see continued

00:13:47.412 --> 00:13:49.580
through a movie or TV event like this.

00:13:49.580 --> 00:13:50.474
- I don't know if this counts

00:13:50.474 --> 00:13:52.543
because technically it came from a movie,

00:13:52.543 --> 00:13:54.632
but I really love 'Buffy
the Vampire Slayer.'

00:13:54.632 --> 00:13:58.742
And I really want a 'Buffy
the Vampire Slayer' movie

00:13:58.742 --> 00:14:01.342
with Sarah Michelle Gellar in the lead,

00:14:01.342 --> 00:14:02.932
like see what's going on with Buffy.

00:14:02.932 --> 00:14:05.078
I just recently watched all of 'Angel,'

00:14:05.078 --> 00:14:06.239
I'm like, in that world.

00:14:06.239 --> 00:14:08.888
I'm like, let's have a Buffy movie again.

00:14:08.888 --> 00:14:10.661
- I feel like a lot of fans
would love to see that.

00:14:10.661 --> 00:14:11.591
- Yeah, that's what I want.

00:14:11.591 --> 00:14:13.710
- Can we just retcon
the last season of 'Lost'

00:14:13.710 --> 00:14:15.650
and then make a movie instead?

00:14:15.650 --> 00:14:18.591
- [Erin] Just pretend
like that never happened?

00:14:18.591 --> 00:14:20.558
- [Naz] I agree with that.

00:14:20.558 --> 00:14:21.878
- We had a Hulk movie come out

00:14:21.878 --> 00:14:22.711
and people were like,

00:14:22.711 --> 00:14:23.808
"Ah, we really liked that Hulk movie,"

00:14:23.808 --> 00:14:25.773
so then we just remade
it three years later.

00:14:25.773 --> 00:14:27.152
Can we do the same thing with 'Lost'?

00:14:27.152 --> 00:14:27.985
- Why not?

00:14:27.985 --> 00:14:29.013
- That's so funny.

00:14:29.013 --> 00:14:32.384
I would love to see 'Fresh
Prince of Bel-Air' get a movie.

00:14:32.384 --> 00:14:33.217
- That's good.

00:14:33.217 --> 00:14:35.373
- I just want to see Carlton and Henry

00:14:35.373 --> 00:14:36.344
who moved to New York.

00:14:36.344 --> 00:14:38.293
Or Carlton went to Princeton,
come back together.

00:14:38.293 --> 00:14:40.624
They should do a Thanksgiving special

00:14:40.624 --> 00:14:42.754
where they all come back
together. So the butler's there.

00:14:42.754 --> 00:14:44.255
And unlike Aaron Paul,

00:14:44.255 --> 00:14:46.404
who looked actually a
lot older in this movie

00:14:46.404 --> 00:14:47.865
but I think it made
sense for his character,

00:14:47.865 --> 00:14:49.673
Will Smith looks the exact same.

00:14:49.673 --> 00:14:52.391
- 'Gemini Man' (all talk over each other)

00:14:52.391 --> 00:14:53.911
- We can just de-age him again.

00:14:53.911 --> 00:14:55.145
- Yeah, just de-age him

00:14:55.145 --> 00:14:57.644
and then we can also get
a 'Family Matters' movie

00:14:57.644 --> 00:14:59.785
that's gonna be all about Carl Winslow,

00:14:59.785 --> 00:15:02.044
then that's gonna tie
us back into 'Die Hard 6'

00:15:02.044 --> 00:15:03.033
when that comes out.

00:15:03.033 --> 00:15:04.576
(women agree)

00:15:04.576 --> 00:15:06.635
- Comments are flying off right now.

00:15:06.635 --> 00:15:07.468
All right guys,

00:15:07.468 --> 00:15:08.843
let's tell the people at home

00:15:08.843 --> 00:15:09.824
what our Fresh Picks are.

00:15:09.824 --> 00:15:11.845
So these are shows, any show on TV,

00:15:11.845 --> 00:15:13.664
that you just think
people should be binging.

00:15:13.664 --> 00:15:14.726
Erin, what's your pick?

00:15:14.726 --> 00:15:18.148
- Mine is 'Dead To Me' on Netflix.

00:15:18.148 --> 00:15:18.981
- [Naz] So good.

00:15:18.981 --> 00:15:20.674
- [Erin] I just, I loved it so much.

00:15:20.674 --> 00:15:23.574
I've never watch something so quickly.

00:15:23.574 --> 00:15:25.516
I just couldn't get enough of it

00:15:25.516 --> 00:15:26.967
and I just had to keep watching it.

00:15:26.967 --> 00:15:28.498
And Christina Applegate,

00:15:28.498 --> 00:15:31.167
Linda Cardellini who I've always loved,

00:15:31.167 --> 00:15:32.378
and then James Marsden.

00:15:32.378 --> 00:15:33.978
I was like, "Yeah bro, come on."

00:15:33.978 --> 00:15:34.811
- I know.

00:15:34.811 --> 00:15:36.286
Also someone who hasn't
been de-aged, right?

00:15:36.286 --> 00:15:37.595
He looks great.
- He looks great.

00:15:37.595 --> 00:15:39.018
- Tall glass of water that man is.

00:15:39.018 --> 00:15:39.989
- Everyone looks great.

00:15:39.989 --> 00:15:40.938
I was like, "Let's watch it."

00:15:40.938 --> 00:15:42.677
And they just started
filming the second season

00:15:42.677 --> 00:15:44.394
and I can't wait to see what happens.

00:15:44.394 --> 00:15:46.869
- Season one ended on the
best cliffhanger ever.

00:15:46.869 --> 00:15:47.702
- So good.

00:15:47.702 --> 00:15:48.535
- Agreed.

00:15:48.535 --> 00:15:49.368
Mark, what do you think?

00:15:49.368 --> 00:15:50.794
- I think we're on season
five of 'Peaky Blinders' now,

00:15:50.794 --> 00:15:51.800
which I haven't seen anything of,

00:15:51.800 --> 00:15:52.633
but now I'm gonna,

00:15:52.633 --> 00:15:53.912
that's my binge movie

00:15:53.912 --> 00:15:55.511
that I'm gonna take the
next month and watch.

00:15:55.511 --> 00:15:58.263
For something that I have been
following every week on HBO,

00:15:58.263 --> 00:16:00.119
'24/7 College Football.'

00:16:00.119 --> 00:16:01.352
They're at a different campus,

00:16:01.352 --> 00:16:03.190
and it's like the college
version of 'Hard Knocks.'

00:16:03.190 --> 00:16:05.472
I think this week is Washington State.

00:16:05.472 --> 00:16:06.790
Their head coach is Mike Leach,

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.832
he's literally an insane human being

00:16:08.832 --> 00:16:10.659
who thinks he's a pirate on the sideline.

00:16:10.659 --> 00:16:12.157
He's gonna get a peg leg installed,

00:16:12.157 --> 00:16:13.509
that's how much he loves it.

00:16:13.509 --> 00:16:15.539
This is gonna be a fun episode to watch.

00:16:15.539 --> 00:16:16.372
- Wow.

00:16:16.372 --> 00:16:18.037
I love seeing real characters like that.

00:16:18.037 --> 00:16:19.221
I love reality.
- Yeah.

00:16:19.221 --> 00:16:21.424
- He's a total nutcase
but a football genius.

00:16:21.424 --> 00:16:22.874
- Okay, I'll definitely
have to check that out.

00:16:22.874 --> 00:16:24.682
So I like to go on a wormholes,

00:16:24.682 --> 00:16:26.529
and after seeing 'El Camino: Breaking Bad'

00:16:26.529 --> 00:16:28.612
I went into a Krysten Ritter wormhole.

00:16:28.612 --> 00:16:29.445
- Ah, yes!

00:16:29.445 --> 00:16:30.314
- And I remembered that

00:16:30.314 --> 00:16:31.767
'Don't Trust the B---- in Apartment 23'

00:16:31.767 --> 00:16:33.881
was one of the first things
I ever watched on Netflix.

00:16:33.881 --> 00:16:35.049
If you guys have never seen it,

00:16:35.049 --> 00:16:35.882
I highly recommend it.

00:16:35.882 --> 00:16:37.111
I went back and started watching it.

00:16:37.111 --> 00:16:39.688
She just plays this
crazy roommate so well.

00:16:39.688 --> 00:16:40.890
And James Van Der Beek's in it

00:16:40.890 --> 00:16:43.450
and he plays a funny
exaggerated version of himself.

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:45.129
I love it. Absolutely love it.
- I do too.

00:16:45.129 --> 00:16:45.962
Oh my God.

00:16:45.962 --> 00:16:47.749
- All right guys, well that is our show.

